THE ACTUAL RAW INPUT WE SHOULD EXPECT FROM MCP SERVER'S "tools/list" for the nimbus cart project

{ "tools": [{
  "name": "slack_post_message",
  "description": "Post a message to a channel",
  "inputSchema": { "type": "object", "properties": { "channel": {"type":"string"}, "text": {"type":"string"} } },
  "annotations": { "readOnlyHint": false, "destructiveHint": false, "openWorldHint": true }
}] }

But there exists some fields which we dont requrie for out project so we are gonna trim them and get a json object with keys - name(important for labelling graph), description
(important for the later automated report).

{ "tools": [{
  "name": "slack_post_message",
  "description": "Post a message to a channel",
}] }

Then we would require an algorithm to be able to assign values to fields like
"blast_radius": { "access": "write", "reaches": "ops_alerts_channel",
                  "sensitivity": 3, "reversibility": 4,
                  "requires_human_approval": false }
access :can be read/write: gives the direction of the directed graph
reaches: gives the neighbour of the current node
sensitivity: an algorithm will assign nodes with a value which represent its damage causing property. like access to important files will have a score 5 (score is from 1-5).
"irreversibility": a number from 1-5 which number closer to 5 means the change is harder to be undone.
"requires_human_approval": tells if there is a human in the loop(a part of future work)

so we have kept this scoring part for phase 3. applying the scoring part on a raw input like 

{ "tools": [{
  "name": "slack_post_message",
  "description": "Post a message to a channel",
  "inputSchema": { "type": "object", "properties": { "channel": {"type":"string"}, "text": {"type":"string"} } },
  "annotations": { "readOnlyHint": false, "destructiveHint": false, "openWorldHint": true }
}] } 

will give a json object like (just an example)

{
  "name": "slack_post_message",
  "description": "Post a message to a channel",
  "inputSchema": { "type": "object" },
  "scopes": ["slack:write"],
  "blast_radius": {
    "access": "write",
    "reaches": "supplier_orders",
    "data_trust": null,
    "requires_human_approval": false,
    "sensitivity": 4,
    "irreversibility": 3
  }
}

then we parse the required data and create a graph using networkX
and then figure out the blast score and attack paths.
